Z||000003||DaRkLoRD||09-20-2001||08:08 PM||||about a year ago I was talking about stereos with Loki.. and he suggested mounting the actual head unit somewhere in the console, but wiring up the faceplate separately, and mounting that on the overhead console.. always thought that was sorta cool...<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||09-20-2001||08:47 PM||||fierosound--Never thought of that.  I plan on a fairly solid ride once I get done.  Does your CD changer skip?  If not, what have you done to prevent that?  Or do you even use the CD changer when you're driving?<P>Steve--I've seen that done on a few custom rides, but was never exactly sure how to do it.  I don't think I could do that to this unit however since it has both a front and a back to the face.  I would have to find a way to keep the motorized faceplate.  How would you even wire something like that up?<P>I'm trying to come up with some ideas for a custom center console (from the dash back to the firewall).  The design I came up with needs to eliminate the head unit from the stock location (no room).  On the other hand, I won't have the p/window switches in the stock location so maybe I could mount it there, at an angle?  Actually, that probably wouldn't work as I don't think there is enough depth.  Hmmm...Any suggestions as to where I could mount the stereo?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Slammed Fiero||09-20-2001||09:22 PM||||Pioneer makes a unit with a remote mount face plate. A friend of mine has one in his caddy. the faceplate is mounted to the sunvisor.<P>Very slicksetup. The headunit itself is hidden under the seat and a CD changer in the console.<P>Expensive though<P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||09-20-2001||09:30 PM||||I'm not planning on buying any more stereo equipment.  I'll use what I have even if that means changing my plans.  I wonder if there's any way to strengthen the stereo to roof mounting.  I was planning on building a housing out of 5/8" MDF (no one around here seems to have 3/4"), then covering it.  I planned on making the box snug around the stereo which should keep it a little more secure, but then heat/circulation might be a problem.  What are everyone's thoughts on the heat/circulation thing?||||reg||1
