Z||000012||gargoyle||09-21-2001||01:46 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">Hey MM, OK let's combine a few of these ideas. How about remote mounting the face and putting the head unit into the center console. This would allow you to really tie the head unit to eliminate skipping. You could then mount the face in the overhead as planned or maybe you would be able to incorporate the face into your new center console since you would need much much depth. As for the heat problem. If you are not using remote amps you will have a problem. I once rigged up a unit into a tank bag for my bike and after awhile it would shut down due to the heat. I found this out once I reached in and burned my fingers on the heat sink. I had to leave the top flap open until I went to just a portable cd player. Hmm, you know I kind of like the head unit into the center console idea. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">OH NO Mr. Bill, please stop me <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000013||Monkeyman||09-21-2001||06:52 PM||||I'm running an amp so I don't think that the head unit is working very hard.  I love the remote mounted faceplate idea, but I don't think I can do that with this stereo.  This head unit has a two sided faceplate.  The front is blacked out.  It comes to life when the stereo is on, but can only be used with the remote.  Also, it's motorized.  The non remote controlled buttons and display are on the backside of the faceplate.  I would need to pull the motor with the faceplate and use that somehow.  Also, I have to push a button on the front of the head unit chassis to eject the CD.  If I mounted it somewhere out of the way (with a stealth cover), I'd still have to find a way to access the eject button.  If not for that, here's what I would envision.  The only hole cut out of whatever covers the head unit would be a small slot for the CD.  I could build a new overhead console (much thinner than I had planned, which is a good thing) and mount the faceplate to the bottom of that, but flush when it was in the "up" position.  Push a button and the faceplate would fold down.  The only problems I see with that are that I still wouldn't have access to the rear of the faceplate (with the other controls) and I don't know what it would look like if I didn't have the faceplate "down".  On the other hand, something like this would eliminate the need for the motor.  Hmmm...This is getting me thinking.  I like that.  Please keep this going so I can figure this out.<P>Another thing to think about is how I can connect the head unit to the faceplate.  I know that something would have to be custom made, but I don't know where I would have it made.  I also wonder about the price of having something made.  If we're looking at $50, that's one thing.  But if this conversion will be more like a few hundred bucks, it's out of my range.<P>Anyone??||||reg||1
Z||000014||Monkeyman||09-22-2001||09:11 AM||||C'mon, guys!  Anyone know someone who could make me something to connect the headunit to the faceplate?  This is starting to sound like a great idea.||||reg||1
Z||000015||BlackDragon||09-22-2001||09:29 AM||||the electronic past shouldn't be that hard, you may be able to do it yourself. the mechanical parts.... well you got me there. i couldn't do that, and dont know anyone who could. sorry. but the electrical part would mainly be extensions, shouldn't be too hard for the individual to throw together  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>good luck, it sounds like a great idea. like i said before, i would rather have mine in the dash. i think it would be cool to do ausi's idea, but i would want the radio facing the other direction....hmmm.....brainstorm.<P>------------------<BR>We are all different for a reason.....<BR>you can read a persons soul through there eyes.....<P><BR>BlackDragon||||reg||1
Z||000016||fierosound||09-22-2001||02:44 PM||||Monkeyman...  My CD Changer never skipped except when crossing railroad tracks.  With the stiffer/lowered suspension, it now skips more often on bad city roads. I may switch to slightly softer front springs in the future. The deck in the dash NEVER skips even now.  I use it when driving in the city, and use the changer on highway drives.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Monkeyman||09-22-2001||03:03 PM||||A CD changer is the my future, but it will be a while.  I'm really thinking hard about this separate faceplate idea.  I'll probably do it if I can figure out the wiring.  I don't want to solder wires onto the stock connections as I might want to put it back together in the future (maybe I'll want to trade up?).  Any of you electrical wizards want to help me figure out how to do this?  I think I can figure out the mechanical part of this fairly easily.  All it would take would be some sort of hinge and a hidden latch.  I'd love to have the faceplate remain motorized, but that's just not going to fly.  Or...here's another idea.  I wonder if I could make something where a pivoting assembly could be hinged.  I can draw what I envision, but I can't quite describe it.  Someone help me out.  Maybe one of you engineers could email me your phone # so I can call and you could draw it in CAD (or something similar).  That way I could post it or forward it for further study.<P>Help me out, guys.  This is keeping me up nights.||||reg||1
Z||000018||8Ball||09-22-2001||06:19 PM||||Ok does your face plate communicate with the head unit thru ribbon cable? The face on my last stereo did. If it does it would be simple to move it to another location just go to radio shack and buy a 15-20 foot section of the ribbon cable you need. Then you can run the ribbon cable under your interior to the head unit.||||reg||1
Z||000019||SLYGUY||09-22-2001||06:36 PM||||My stock stereo was sick. Had to replace it. I found a good deal on a Kenwood detachable face unit. It has a detachable face and comes with a 5 disc changer. <P>Original OEM stereo made the left dash speaker sound blown, it wasn't. Also, there was nothing coming from the rear speakers. Had the head unit checked and the rear channel was blown. <P>Replaced the rear 4x6s with Kenwoods. Still have to replace dash speakers. <P>Anyone know if putting that changer in the front compartment is a good idea. I don't want heat, or rain, or dust to destroy it but right now it is mounted to the passenger floorboard preventing floormats and suicidally close to the heater core when/if that ruptures.<P>Where can I put speakers to get decent base?<P>Your help is appreciated.<P>Sly<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/SLYGUYSIG.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000020||SLYGUY||09-22-2001||06:37 PM||||Bass actually.<P>You kow what I mean.<P>LOL<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000021||Loki||09-22-2001||06:50 PM||||To connect the faceplate to the chassis all you have to do is get a good length of the ribbon wire, how ever many wires to the contacts between the face and chassis. Solder a wire to each contact on the face and then the same connector/wire on the chassis. There is also usually a little button on the chassis that has to be "in" so the unit thinks the face is "on" so it will power up. also if mount the chassis someplace in the car and would like to hide it but still use the cd plyer (if one is equipped) you have to wire a button up to the eject button if it isnt electric from the face. Alot arnt. Also hope you dont care about a warrenty   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif">   <BR>ICQ# 16477998<BR>http://members.home.net/fieroloki||||reg||1
Z||000022||Monkeyman||09-22-2001||07:37 PM||||I couldn't care less about the warranty.  I've had the head unit about a year, so the warranty is probably expired anyway.  There isn't any button (AFAIK) to tell the head unit that the faceplate is attached as it's not removable.  I'll have to check to see how the two are connected.  (It'll be a *****  just trying to get the faceplate off as it's motorized.)  I don't want to solder anything to the contacts if I can help it as I may want to put the stereo back together someday if I want to sell it (to buy another).  If that's what I have to do, I may just try and sell this one now and buy up while I have a chance.  <P>I just looked at the stereo.  There is a small ribbon cable attaching the headunit to the faceplate, but it's awfully small.  I know that I couldn't do the soldering, but there's probably someone in town who can.  On the other hand, I paid extra money for this one because of some of the neat features that I obviously won't be using now such as the motorized, two sided faceplate, etc.||||reg||1
Z||000023||8Ball||09-22-2001||08:31 PM||||You could keep the motorized faceplate if you wish to take the time to remove the assembly for the motor and faceplate. Wouldn't be that hard just painstakeing.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Monkeyman||09-22-2001||10:01 PM||||The motor would go the wrong direction.  I think I found another stereo that will fit the bill.  Now I just have to sell the one I've got.  See the Mall in the next day or so for more info on that.<P>Here's a question about the new stereo I'm considering.  It's the Sony ES CDX-CA860X.  It has a blacked out face (similar to mine), but it's completely controlled by the remote(s).  One of the remotes is the Rotary Commander.  It uses IR signals.  Are IR signals omni directional?  Seems like it would be since you can mount the remote anywhere and still be able to control the head unit.  But...The specs say something about being able to "change the direction of the infrared rays emitted by the" remote.  Almost makes it sound like it's a line-of-sight thing.  Anyone know the answer?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/PontiacAPE.jpg">    <BR>Monkeyman||||reg||1
Z||000025||Doni Hagan||09-22-2001||10:13 PM||||One may wish to start such a fabrication with an overhead console from a minivan, some plastic sheeting and a big bottle of Crazy Glue. <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Jam004_2.jpg"> <P>Inspired by one of Bill Osmer's pristine modifications, I recently installed a Dodge minivan console in place of the stock one w/subwoofer control. It looks stock and there's enough clearance at the front end to theoretically scratchbuild a box to mount a stereo in. RE: support... Why wouldn't the crossmember the stock console was mounted to prove strong enough to handle the additional stress?<p>[This message has been edited by Doni Hagan (edited 09-22-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000026||Doni Hagan||09-22-2001||10:52 PM||||^^||||reg||1
Z||000027||Monkeyman||09-22-2001||11:02 PM||||Although I love Bills ideas (he has some neat little toys, doesn't he?), I have a sunroof so I think I can probably build what I need easier than I could modify something from another vehicle.  Good idea, though.  This is the kind of input I'm looking for.  Stuff to make me think.||||reg||1
Z||000028||gargoyle||09-23-2001||02:28 AM||||Yes IR is line of site but you can bounce it off of surfaces to redirect the signal. So you could have your unit behind you and bounce the signal off the dash. I did this with a friends small home unit. Her deck was in the kitchen out of sight but facing the back wall. She would just point the remote at the back wall to control it, worked pretty well.||||reg||1
Z||000029||Monkeyman||09-23-2001||06:42 AM||||I would need to figure out how to make it work with the faceplate in both the up (flush with the bottom of the upper light console) and the lowered positions.  Hmm...Oh, well.  Minor detail.  I still need to figure out a way to make the connections without making them permanent.  Since this new stereo I'm interested in has a detachable face, I'm pretty sure it doesn't use a ribbon cable, just those open contacts.  The connection at the head unit (probably like Aus' suggested) doesn't have to look pretty as it will be hidden, but the connection at the faceplate needs to be nice.  I also need to figure out the mechanics of the flip down faceplate.||||reg||1
Z||000030||Oreif||09-23-2001||09:01 AM||||First, You can mount the head unit above. You will need to get a fiberglass headliner board. Cut out the area where you want the radio to go and mount it to the exsisiting overhead console mounts. If you want to reduce skipping of the CD player use cushioned mounts. You then need to make the cover over the radio with fiberglass and attach it to the headboard. You can attach the lights to the bottom of the fiberglass or if you have some room sink them into it.<BR>  ||||reg||1
Z||000031||Monkeyman||09-23-2001||09:46 AM||||Cushioned mounts?  Just pieces of rubber or were you thinking of something else?<P>I've never made anything out of fiberglass before.  What about molded ABS?  Would that work as well?  My best friend is an R&D/prototyper with access to a plastic molding machine.  I know he could do it.  I'd just need to make the wooden mold (a tough enough job for me, but probably easier than working with fiberglass).<P>If I made the pod out of MDF, is there anything I could cover it with so the fiberglass wouldn't stick to it?  Then remove the MDF base from the new Fiberglass mold?  (Can you tell I've never done anything like this before?)||||reg||1
Z||000032||Oreif||09-23-2001||01:44 PM||||JCWhitney has some units made for vans to put stereo's and CB radios up on top. You could probably look in their catalog and get a basic Idea what you want then just make it out of wood. Once it's covered no one will know if it's wood or fiberglass. You might even be able to find one that you can cut-out the stereo section and just blend it in to the exsisting headliner.<BR>As for shock mounts, yes a small set of rubber bushings.||||reg||1
